{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1785185830,"nonce":"uJoB3UPui68wOkOyePVG7WJR+os=","title":"ADR-0062: NFS-sourced PVs need an NFS client on every Step node — state the prerequisite, unblock the dogfood","message":"The delivery path had never been mounted end-to-end. When red-teamed it got all the way through admission and scheduling and then failed in kubelet: *\"bad option; ... you might need a /sbin/mount.nfs helper program\"*, and the Pod hung in `ContainerCreating` **forever** — no crash, no event a user would read as fatal.\n\nThe colima node has no `nfs-utils`. The kernel client is fine; the userspace helper is missing.\n\nSo ADR-0062's \"the Step Pod needs nothing, kubelet does the mount\" is true of **privilege** and false of **packages**. This is not a DaemonSet and not something Scarab installs, but it is a **per-node prerequisite** and the ADR must say so plainly. Present on most distributions; its absence on minimal images (Bottlerocket, hardened AMIs) is a real risk worth naming rather than discovering.\n\nAlso blocks `b5e5d40` (s9): the spike cannot run on `just up` or `local-helm` until the colima VM has `nfs-common`.\n\n\n- [ ] `deploy/local-proc` and `deploy/local-helm` provision an NFS client on the node, via a `just` recipe rather than manual steps\n- [ ] The prerequisite is documented in ADR-0062's Consequences and in the deployment docs, next to the honest comparison ADR-0061 already makes against Woodpecker's \"only a default StorageClass\"\n- [ ] A missing NFS client surfaces as a **named, fast** failure, not a Pod wedged in ContainerCreating indefinitely\n- [ ] The substrate recipe checks for the helper and says so\n\n\nNone - can start immediately","files":null}]}